<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leutkirch im Allgäu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leutkirch im <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a> is a town in the Allgäu region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> that grew up as the market centre of the Nibelgau district. It was a free imperial city from the 1290s until 1802, a small council-governed town living from linen weaving, and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and then, in 1810, to Württemberg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leutkirch-im-allgau.jpg" alt="Leutkirch im Allgäu" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flodur63 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leutkirch_-_Schloss_Zeil_-_Leutkirch_v_N_08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Leutkirch im <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a> is a former Free Imperial City in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. It lies in the western Allgäu, in the district of Ravensburg and the administrative region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, and the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> are about 40 km away. The name comes from Leutekirche, 'church of the people', the church now called Sankt Martin. The town grew from the merger of the villages of Ufhofen and Mittelhofen, and the first mention of its church is in a document of the Abbey of St. Gall from 766.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1293 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-of-nassau">Adolf of Nassau</a> granted Leutkirch the right to rule itself under the town code of Lindau, effectively making it a Free Imperial City. A bailiff appointed by the king still ruled it for a while, a town council is first mentioned in 1311, and from the 15th century an elected mayor chaired it. The main industry was the linen trade, and the weavers' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> once had 200 members. The town joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> in 1488 and gained a seat and vote in it and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>.</p><p>Leutkirch became officially Lutheran in 1546 and later joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>. A dispute with the abbot of Weingarten Abbey over St. Martin's parish church ended in a compromise in 1562: the Catholics kept the parish church and the Protestants took the hospital church, expanded in 1589 and now called the Memorial Church. The town suffered heavily in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and its population fell drastically. A baroque town hall was built in 1740. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> it remained one of the smallest of the fifty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">Free Imperial Cities</a>.</p><p>In the mediatisation of the early 1800s the town lost its independence. It was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and in 1810 became part of the Kingdom of Württemberg. The surrounding countryside, the Free Men of the Leutkircher Heath, was a Free Imperial Village territory and shared its fate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since the municipal reform of 1972 the urban area includes eight former municipalities, among them Diepoldshofen, Friesenhofen and Wuchzenhofen. The Museum im Bock shows the history of the town and its craftsmen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeil-castle">Schloss Zeil</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> castle begun in 1599 by Truchsess Froben von Waldburg-Zeil after he demolished the medieval castle, stands five kilometres away; he built its church and monastery first. The sculptor and painter Hans Multscher, about 1400–1467, was born in the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leutkirch im Allgäu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Leutkirch</b> grew up as the market centre of the Nibelgau in upper Swabia.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-leutkirch">Free Imperial City of Leutkirch</a></li></ul></section>
